{
  "image_name": "3bba0133a9d57cccbda5979af5204c52.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a gut–brain–immune circuit linking psychological stress to intestinal inflammation and systemic effects. At the top, the brain is connected to the gut, indicating bidirectional communication during a stress response. In the intestine, stress appears to alter interactions among the gut microbiota, epithelial cells, immune cells, and the enteric nervous system.\n\nThe epithelial barrier produces antimicrobial peptides and responds to IL-22, which is shown coming from ILC3 cells. These immune cells are influenced by enteric neurons through RET/GFL signaling, suggesting that neural activity helps maintain mucosal defense. A second pathway shows TH2 cells releasing IL-4 and IL-5, promoting mast cells and eosinophils. Mast cells release histamine, while eosinophils release granule proteins, both of which likely act on local neurons and contribute to gut dysfunction or inflammation.\n\nAdrenergic signaling also appears in the diagram: noradrenaline acting through β2AR is linked to enteric neurons and M2 macrophages, suggesting stress-related neural signals shape anti- or pro-inflammatory immune behavior in deeper gut layers. Finally, cytokine-producing cells release inflammatory mediators such as IL-1β, TNF, and IL-6 into the systemic circulation, indicating that local gut stress responses can spread throughout the body.",
